---
/**
 * The citation block on a guide.
 *
 * CONTENT_BRIEF: "Every factual, legal or regulatory claim carries a source and
 * a review date." This component is how that shows up for the reader rather
 * than only in a repo file — the sources are visible, linked and dated, and the
 * review date is stated rather than implied.
 *
 * It is deliberately plain. A citation block that looks like a design element
 * reads as decoration; one that looks like a citation block reads as evidence.
 */
interface Source {
  label: string;
  url: string;
  read: Date;
}
interface Props {
  sources: Source[];
  reviewBy: Date;
  legalDisclaimer?: boolean;
}
import { langForPath } from '../lib/offer';
const { sources, reviewBy, legalDisclaimer = false } = Astro.props;

/* Spanish from the path, like the FAQ heading. The source TITLES are never
   translated — a citation names a publication as its publisher named it, and
   a translated title is one a reader cannot search for — so the Spanish block
   says in one line that the sources are in English. */
const es = langForPath(Astro.url.pathname) === 'es';
const fmt = (d: Date) =>
  d.toLocaleDateString(es ? 'es-US' : 'en-US', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
---
{/* THE WHOLE SECTION IS BOILERPLATE TO THE HARNESS, added 10 Sep 2026, and the
    reason is that a bibliography is not body prose and both checks were
    treating it as though it were.

    WHAT WENT WRONG. Three new ant pages were written from the same regional
    extension publication — which is correct, it is the authoritative source for
    ant identification in this region — and the duplicate-sentence scanner
    failed them, because the citation title is a 10+ word string appearing on
    three or more pages. The scanner was doing exactly what it was built to do.
    It was pointed at the wrong text.

    Two pages citing the same publication is not duplicated content. It is two
    pages honestly citing the same publication, and the alternative the check
    was pushing toward — paraphrasing a title so it looks different — would be
    worse in every respect.

    IT ALSO STOPS COUNTING TOWARD THE WORD FLOOR, which is the same argument
    from the other side. The M1 floor is meant to measure how much a page
    genuinely says. A citation list inflates that by thirty to seventy words
    without adding anything a reader came for, and a page should not reach three
    thousand words partly on the strength of its own footnotes.

    Four pages dipped under the floor when this was applied and were written up
    rather than exempted. That is the correct direction: the check got more
    honest and the pages got longer, instead of the pages staying the same and
    the number getting easier. */}
<section class="sources" data-boilerplate>
  <h2>{es ? 'Fuentes' : 'Sources'}</h2>
  {es && <p>Las fuentes están publicadas en inglés por las instituciones que las escribieron.</p>}

  {legalDisclaimer && es && (
    <p class="disclaimer" data-boilerplate>
      <strong>Esto es información, no asesoría legal.</strong> Somos una
      empresa de control de plagas, no abogados. Lo que sigue es nuestra
      lectura de reglas publicadas, con enlaces para que usted mismo las
      revise. Para asesoría sobre su situación, hable con un abogado, con
      <a href="https://www.washingtonlawhelp.org/" rel="nofollow noopener">WashingtonLawHelp</a>,
      o con la oficina de cumplimiento de códigos de su ciudad o condado.
    </p>
  )}
  {legalDisclaimer && !es && (
    <p class="disclaimer" data-boilerplate>
      <strong>This is information, not legal advice.</strong> We are a pest
      control company, not attorneys. What follows is our reading of published
      rules, with links so you can check them yourself. For advice on your own
      situation, talk to a lawyer, to
      <a href="https://www.washingtonlawhelp.org/" rel="nofollow noopener">WashingtonLawHelp</a>,
      or to your city or county code enforcement office.
    </p>
  )}

  <ul>
    {sources.map((s) => (
      <li>
        <a href={s.url} rel="nofollow noopener" lang={es ? 'en' : undefined}>{s.label}</a>
        <span class="read">{es ? ` — consultada el ${fmt(s.read)}` : ` — read ${fmt(s.read)}`}</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  {es ? (
    <p class="review" data-boilerplate>
      La información cambia. Esta página tiene programada una revisión contra
      sus fuentes a más tardar el <strong>{fmt(reviewBy)}</strong>. Si la está
      leyendo después de esa fecha y algo no coincide con lo que dice la fuente
      actual, la fuente tiene la razón y nosotros no — por favor díganoslo.
    </p>
  ) : (
    <p class="review" data-boilerplate>
      Rules change. This page is scheduled for review against its sources by
      <strong>{fmt(reviewBy)}</strong>. If you are reading it after that date and
      something looks out of step with the current rule, the rule is right and we
      are wrong — please tell us.
    </p>
  )}
</section>

{/* No scoped <style> here, on purpose. This block used to carry its own rules
    built out of currentColor and opacity — the one piece of the page the
    design tokens could not reach, and it drifted. It is styled in §17 of
    src/styles/global.css with the same inks, rules and advisory treatment as
    every other callout, so the citation block, the legal disclaimer and the
    archived-post banner read as one system instead of three. */}
